  data:  # 5b4c
    kind: invariant  # 5b4c
    title: "A module hint BYPASSES the ambiguous_names gate in io_boundary lookup — a wrong receiver type yields a confident misclassification, re-opening the WI-razol FP cascade"  # 5b4c
    status: violated  # 5b4c
    priority: 1  # 5b4c
    parent:  # 5b4c
    tags:  # 5b4c
    - io_boundary  # 5b4c
    - analysis_quality  # 5b4c
    - taint  # 5b4c
    isbefore: []  # 5b4c
    duplicate_of: []  # 5b4c
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5b4c
    pr_ref:  # 5b4c
    description: "A MODULE HINT DISABLES THE ambiguous_names GATE, so a WRONG receiver type\nproduces a CONFIDENT boundary classification instead of degrading to silence.\n\nMECHANISM (io_boundary.py:568-579): when a module hint is supplied, the lookup\nreturns filtered[0] and NEVER reaches gate_named_entry. gate_named_entry is\nwhat enforces the INV-tapat/INV-maluk rule. So the hint does not merely ENABLE\nclassification -- it BYPASSES the protection.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS NOW. pathlib.Path catalogues `replace` and `rename`, and BOTH\nare in python.yaml's ambiguous_names. Any receiver-typing work (WI-zilag and\neverything downstream of INV-linub) supplies exactly such a hint, so it\nre-opens the WI-razol \"5541-FP cascade\" that taint.py:619-621 closed:\n\n    p = Path(raw); p = str(p); p.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\")\n\nbecomes an fs_write io-boundary AND -- because taint.py auto-derives sinks\nfrom io_primitives (WI-lokuv) -- a host_fs/untrusted TAINT SINK. A plain\nstr.replace reported as a filesystem write, in the expensive direction for a\nsecurity tool.\n\nMEASURED, adversarially, on fixtures: the bare-name dict form\n(EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES[\"Path\"]=\"pathlib.Path\", which does NO import\ncheck) turns `from fastapi import Path; item.replace(...)` into a reported\nfs_write -- base 0 boundaries, patched 1, wrong.\n\nTHE REGRESSION TEST STAYS GREEN WHILE THE INVARIANT IS DEFEATED.\ntest_io_boundary.py:1687 hand-constructs the `external` dst, so it never\nexercises the hinted path. A passing suite is NOT evidence of safety here --\nwhich is exactly the reassurance a receiver-typing PR would invite.\n\nTHIS IS PRE-EXISTING AND INDEPENDENT of any recall change. It should be fixed\nFIRST, because every planned receiver-typing improvement is unsafe until it\nis: the whole point of typing a receiver is to supply the hint that currently\nswitches the gate off.\n\nDIRECTION: fixing it makes classification STRICTER (fewer boundaries), so it\nis the safe direction, but it must be measured on findings both ways --\nsome current classifications may depend on the bypass."  # 5b4c
    fields:  # 5b4c
      statement: "Supplying a module hint to the io-boundary lookup MUST NOT bypass the ambiguous_names / gate_named_entry protection. A receiver type that is wrong must degrade to silence, never to a confident boundary classification."  # 5b4c
      root_cause: "io_boundary.py:568-579 returns filtered[0] on the module-hint path and never calls gate_named_entry, so INV-tapat/INV-maluk enforcement is skipped exactly when a hint is present."  # 5b4c
- op: update  # f967
  at: '2026-08-07T10:20:25Z'  # f967
  by: agent  # f967
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f967
  clock: 2  # f967
  nonce: f967  # f967
  set:  # f967
    title: "A bare constructor name is emitted as receiver evidence with no binding check, so a rebound name mints a confident wrong I/O classification"  # f967
    description: "A bare constructor name is emitted as receiver evidence with NO check on what\nthat name is bound to in the file, so a rebound name mints a confident, wrong\nI/O classification.\n\nMECHANISM (py.py, `_external_constructor_module`). The function has two\nbranches. The `ast.Attribute` branch (`socket.socket(...)`) verifies its base\nname against `module_imports` before trusting it. The `ast.Name` branch beside\nit verified nothing: it looked the bare identifier up in\nEXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES and emitted the catalogued module as the receiver\ntype of whatever the call returned.\n\nLIVE REPRO on the shipping tree, no monkeypatch, no configuration:\n\n    from decoy_lib import open\n    def handler(spec):\n        v = open(spec)\n        return v.read()          # reported fs_read against `file.read`\n\n`tag_io_boundaries` returns 1. The unshadowed twin returns 2, so this is a\nfalse positive rather than a dead analyzer. A filesystem read invented for an\nobject that is not a file — the expensive direction for a security tool.\n\n=== THE ORIGINAL FILING'S REMEDY WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND IS WITHDRAWN ===\n\nThis item was filed with `statement`: \"Supplying a module hint to the\nio-boundary lookup MUST NOT bypass the ambiguous_names / gate_named_entry\nprotection.\" That remedy cannot be implemented. `gate_named_entry` vetoes any\nname in `ambiguous_names` AND any `call_construct == \"method\"`, and 48 of\npython.yaml's 222 primitives (21.6%) have an ambiguous short name — including\nsocket.socket.send/recv/connect, file.read/write, asyncio.StreamReader.read,\npathlib.Path.replace/rename and every django.db.models entry, i.e. precisely\nthe method-kind entries receiver typing exists to reach.\n\nMEASURED A/B, 8 repos (pretix, sqlalchemy, mitmproxy, kserve, poetry,\nprometheus, caddy, alertmanager), production `cmd_io_boundaries` under an\nin-process monkeypatch, positive-controlled in both directions:\n\n  - 61.5% of what the CLI prints by default destroyed (3812 -> 1468)\n  - 87.2% of the JSON `total_io_edges` field destroyed (2689 -> 345)\n  - ZERO gained, at every repo and every primitive\n  - subprocess -> 0 in 5 of 8 repos; every boundary type -> 0 in caddy\n  - 92.9% of the loss comes from the `call_construct == \"method\"` clause,\n    NOT the ambiguity clause this item's title names\n  - Go is hit on FUNCTION-kind entries with correct package hints\n    (io.ReadAll, os.ReadFile, os/exec.Command) because `pkg.Func()` is a\n    selector expression threaded as call_construct=\"method\"\n  - no single clause is safe: the cheapest (ambiguous_names only) costs 37.8%\n\nThe gate's own docstring says it \"is reached only when there is no usable\nmodule hint\". Clause 1 is PREMISED on hint-absence. Applying it when a hint is\npresent is a category error.\n\n=== ALSO CORRECTED: THE FILING'S WORKED EXAMPLE ===\n\nThe description claimed a measured FP from `EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES[\"Path\"]`\n= \"pathlib.Path\". That key has never been in the table — the table is\n{\"open\": \"file\", \"socket.socket\": \"socket.socket\"}. The measurement ADDED it.\nSo the FP as described does not exist on the shipping tree; it is a forecast\nabout receiver-typing work, and it is pinned as a test on that basis.\n\nWhy the distinction matters: across 5 Python repos there are 467 bare\n`Path(...)` call sites and ALL 467 are import-bound to pathlib. The filed\nremedy refuses 467/467. The shipped fix refuses 0/467 and refuses only the\n`from fastapi import Path` site. The two remedies differ on precision, not on\nstrictness.\n\n=== WHY THE FIX IS AT THE PRODUCER ===\n\nThe lying edge and the honest edge are byte-identical on arrival: same\ncall_construct, same resolution_quality (`type_inferred`), same shape. No\nconsumer can separate them, because the evidence that would — what the name is\nbound to in this file — is destroyed upstream. One producer fix closes both\nconsumers (io-boundary and taint), verified on both.\n\n=== RESOLUTION ===\n\n`_import_binding_for(name, imports, module_imports)` is now the single answer\nto \"does an import in this file rebind this bare name, and to what?\", consumed\nby `_external_constructor_module` (new gate) and `_receiver_type_id_trustworthy`\n(behaviour-identical refactor of the inline test it already had). A binding to\nanything other than the claimed module withholds the hint; the edge degrades to\n`external` and every consumer refuses it as an untyped method call. Unbound\nstays trusted, because for a bare name that means the builtin.\n\n`io_boundary._module_matches` was specified for the comparison and REJECTED on\nmeasurement: it returns True for `mylib.pathlib.Path` against `pathlib.Path`\nand for `mypkg.file` against `file`, i.e. it trusts exactly the vendored\nbindings this guard exists to refuse. It is permissive by design because it\nanswers a different question (hint compatibility, not binding identity).\n\nDIRECTION: strictly stricter; the change can only withhold. Corpus delta today\nis 0 boundaries in either direction (330 bare `open` sites across 5 Python\nrepos, 0 import-shadowed), with three positive controls proving the shadow\ndetectors fire. The gain is the one real FP above plus the future population.\n\nPARITY TEST: enumerates EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES itself, deriving fixture\nmethod names from python.yaml, so a future key auto-generates its own shadow\nfixture. Every negative assertion is paired with a positive control — this\nitem's own pre-existing regression test (test_io_boundary.py:1687) stayed green\nthrough the entire defect because it hand-constructs its `external` dst instead\nof running the analyzer.\n\nDISCLOSED GAPS: def/assign shadowing out of scope (import binding only,\nmatching WI-supat D3; measured 0 for `open` today). `from io import open` is\nrefused — a conservative false negative, since the claimed module `file` is\nsynthetic and no import path can equal it. Aliased imports miss the table by\nkey and mint nothing. Relative imports, importlib, conditional and\nTYPE_CHECKING imports are unprobed. No other analyzer has an\nEXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES analogue, so a cross-language parity test would\nassert nothing today and is not built."  # f967
    fields:  # f967
      statement: "A bare identifier used as evidence of a constructed object's type MUST be confirmed still bound to the module the constructor table claims before that module is emitted as receiver evidence. A mismatched binding must degrade to silence, never to a confident boundary classification."  # f967
      root_cause: "py.py _external_constructor_module's ast.Name branch looked the bare identifier up in EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES with no binding check, while the ast.Attribute branch beside it verified its base against module_imports. The original filing named the io_boundary.py:568-579 ambiguous_names bypass instead; that mechanism is real but its remedy is self-defeating (measured: 61.5%-87.2% of reported boundaries destroyed, zero gained) and is withdrawn."  # f967
